Chapter: RocTagListPayload
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Ported from roc-lang/roc, src/eval/test/eval_closure_recursion_tests.zig,
 "inspect: tag with list payload". The whole Roc case:

     match Some([1, 2]) { Some(lst) => match lst { [a, b] => a + b, _ => 0 }, None => 0 }

 and Roc expects 3.

 A LIST INSIDE A TAG PAYLOAD, read back out by matching the tag. Roc's `Some`
 is a structural tag needing no declaration; Codex wants a declared sum type,
 so the port declares one. That is the adaptation with the most in it and it
 is named here rather than buried: the Codex version tests a payload of a
 DECLARED parametric type, which is a stronger statement than Roc's, not a
 weaker one -- a declared `Holder (a)` is exactly the shape whose type
 argument a substitution walk has to be able to see into.

 The constructor is `Held`/`Nothing-Held` rather than `Some`/`None` because
 `Some` is already spoken for in the depot's own tests and shadowing it would
 test the name.

 Adaptations: list patterns become `list-at`, and Roc's `3.0` is Codex's `3`.

Section: Types

  Holder (a) = | Held (a) | NotHeld

Section: Functions

  read-held : Holder (List Integer) -> Integer
  read-held (h) =
   when h
    is Held (lst) -> list-at lst 0 + list-at lst 1
    is NotHeld -> 0

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni (show (read-held (Held [1, 2])))
  end
